course: dictionary
lesson: 13493
title: Illuminate: light, clarity, and gold
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Illuminate is a verb. Its most common meaning is to light something up, so that we can see it. «The torch illuminated the dark cave.» The torch gave light, and the cave became easy to see. Ask yourself: what is the light doing?
explain: Illuminate can also be a formal word for making something clearer and easier to understand. «The teacher's drawing illuminated the hard idea.» Nobody shone a light on the idea. The drawing made the idea clear, so the children understood it.
explain: Long ago, monks decorated books with bright pictures and gold. These books are called illuminated manuscripts. So when you see illuminated in a history text, check the clues: old books, pictures and gold tell you the meaning is about decoration, not light.
fact: Illuminate means to light something up or to shine light on it.
fact: In formal writing, illuminate means to make something clearer and easier to understand.
fact: An illuminated manuscript is an old book decorated with bright pictures and gold.
fact: Phrases to learn: «brightly illuminated» and «illuminate the room».
fact: In science, the Sun illuminates the Moon, so we can see it shining in the sky.
exercise: «The torch illuminated the dark cave.» What does illuminated mean? | Sang loudly | *Lit up | Broke | Made dark
exercise: «The teacher's drawing illuminated the hard idea.» What does illuminated mean here? | Hid | Painted it black | Forgot | *Made clearer and easier to understand
exercise: Find the verb in this sentence: «The Sun illuminates the Moon.» | Sun | Moon | *illuminates | Shine
test: «Monks used gold and bright colours to illuminate the old books.» What does illuminate mean here? | *Decorate with bright pictures and gold | Lose | Burn | Copy in pencil
test: Mia says, «The torch illuminates the object so we can see it.» What is she saying? | *It lights the object so we can see it | It breaks the object | It makes the object heavier | It hides the object
exam: «This example illuminates the problem.» Which meaning fits best? | It makes the problem wet | *It makes the problem clearer and easier to understand | It makes the problem disappear | It makes the problem bigger
